This paper studies stochastic contextual bandits with knapsack constraints (CBwK) in the small budget regime where the initial budget $B = \Omega(\sqrt{T})$. The authors propose the Adaptive and Universal Primal--Dual (AUPD) algorithm, which achieves $	ilde{O}((1 +  rac{
u^*}{\delta b})\sqrt{T})$ regret under the strict feasibility assumption without requiring prior knowledge of the feasibility margin. In the absence of this assumption, AUPD achieves $	ilde{O}(\sqrt{T} +  rac{
u^*}{\sqrt{b}}T^{ rac{3}{4}})$ regret, which is the first such result in the literature. The algorithm uses a novel Lyapunov drift analysis to control virtual queues that track over-consumption, enabling adaptive budget-aware decision-making.
